The qualified two-pass tool model was tested with two read-only interfaces outside its training demonstrations. A question succeeded only if the model selected the right data, received the corresponding observation and returned a correct answer. All turns, including invalid calls, counted toward elapsed time.

*Human database questions.* For each of 24 WikiSQL test tables we exposed only its human-readable columns and a typed aggregate-and-filter interface [14]. Four tables represented each of six operations. The runtime bound model-supplied values as SQLite parameters. The scorer executed the last call again on a fixed row bootstrap and compared both denotations against the publisher query. The trained model finished 15 answers and supplied one correct grounded result in 18.42 s. Base finished two and supplied zero correct grounded results in 135.09 s. Eight candidate requests ended in invalid calls. For example, when asked to count starting grids for a particular driver, the model counted all 19 rows and accurately reported the wrong result. The missing driver filter changed the meaning of the query.

*Live package dependencies.* Fourteen requests named installed Python distributions and asked for the version of the alphabetically first or last active runtime dependency. The model had to read real dependency metadata, select a name from that observation, read its installed version and report it. The candidate completed 12 answers, gave four correct version strings and satisfied the full path on three requests. Base satisfied none. The comparison took 16.92 versus 53.05 s (three paired gains and zero losses, $p=0.25$). On the Pydantic request the model read the dependency list, then looked up the version of Pydantic itself. These tests separate calling a valid function from choosing the arguments that answer the user’s question.

#figure(image("field-performance.svg", width: 99%), caption: [Complete-session speed and paired accuracy. BFCL, database and package tasks require grounded execution. HotpotQA scores the reference phrase in a completed answer. Word problems score the visible numerical answer regardless of call use. Each line compares the same held-out requests with the same generation policy.])

*Human arithmetic with an actual calculator.* The training set contained 640 GSM8K solutions whose annotated arithmetic could be executed exactly. The model learned a call and an observation-conditioned answer for each, plus 512 rehearsal turns. Both models received the same calculator and budget on 24 ASDiv and 24 SVAMP questions. The candidate returned 17 correct grounded answers versus zero for Base. When we credited any completed final numeric answer, Base won 20 to 17. ASDiv favored the candidate 11 to 8 and SVAMP favored Base 12 to 6. The paired final-answer comparison had 11 gains and 14 losses ($p=0.690$). The models completed 47 and 21 answers in 25.52 and 209.90 s respectively. In one human problem, a farmer had 90 tomatoes and picked 154 yesterday and 50 today. Asked how many were picked, the candidate added all three quantities and reported 294. The calculator executed the requested program exactly. The question called for 204. This fit failed the preregistered numeric-content and source-retention gates.

*Training on human table queries.* We continued the typed adapter on 768 different WikiSQL training tables balanced across six operations, with tool rehearsal. On 36 previously unenrolled test tables the model completed eight grounded queries against zero for Base (eight gains, no losses, exact $p=0.00781$). Per six requests it solved one lookup, one maximum, three minima, one count, zero sums and two averages. It retained 19/22 public numerical executions. Across all 58 sessions, total time was 63.27 versus 285.42 s and peak MLX allocation 1.08 versus 1.66 GiB. On a human question asking for total picks, it counted matching rows instead of summing the selected column. Correct syntax and an executed database statement did not ensure the right operation. The per-operation floor closed deployment.

*Learning to choose operands.* The second calculator study represents a program as an operator and two pointers per step, following the use of executable operation programs in prior semantic parsers [20]. A pointer can select a number in the request, one of twelve constants or an earlier result. Frozen Qwen prefix activations encode the question. One native attention layer with shared rank-eight projection adapters runs twice at every step. The model chooses when to stop, up to six steps. Its 897,029 trainable parameters received 1,500 source-balanced updates on 1,024 GSM8K and 850 MAWPS training questions [21]. All 1,874 training programs executed to their reference values. The model can express incorrect but syntactically valid mathematics. On a held-out grouping question with ten boys and fifteen girls, its program added the counts and divided by ten. Its free response nevertheless gave the correct five groups. Only the answer-content endpoint credited this response.

On 48 new questions the planner produced 19 correct grounded answers, against zero for ordinary Base. It returned 20 correct final numbers against 28, with eight paired gains and sixteen losses (exact $p=0.152$). It lost four net answers in each source, 9/24 versus 13/24 on ASDiv and 11/24 versus 15/24 on SVAMP. Actual session time fell from 182.43 to 11.00 s and peak MLX memory from 1.66 to 1.16 GiB. Forty of the 48 chosen programs used a single operation. The registered final-answer gate closed the matched-depth follow-up.

*Human dialogue and a changed inventory.* We continued the typed adapter on 384 MultiWOZ 2.2 training dialogues equally divided between hotels and restaurants, mixed with 512 rehearsal examples. The held-out panel contained eight hotel, eight restaurant and twelve attraction dialogues. We appended a count request to each human conversation and ran it against two genuine database inventories. The same initial prompt IDs have different correct counts. The model must select every current preference, call SQLite and report the result in both worlds. The first inventory and the reduced inventory are a single paired test per dialogue. [18]

The fit solved both inventories for seven of 28 dialogues against zero for Base (seven paired gains, no losses, exact $p=0.0156$). Its service totals were 2/8 hotels, 5/8 restaurants and 0/12 attractions. It also solved 20/22 public numerical executions against zero. Relative to the earlier typed model on these 22, it gained three and lost none. Across the 78 individual sessions it scored 34 versus 0, completed 78 versus 14 and took 78.84 versus 299.61 s. On the attraction question about All Saints Church, the model called SQLite with hotel, star, price and food conditions. The database faithfully returned zero. The target included a match. The hotel and held-out attraction floors closed promotion despite the overall time and accuracy gains.

*Two actual user messages.* The public conversation interface carries visible assistant replies, calls and observed results into a subsequent user message. We sent eight missing-name requests and eight references to earlier live package listings through the qualified tool weights and untouched Base. The recurrent system completed 7/8 clarifications, against 0/8 for Base. Its final text contained the correct version in 13/16 exchanges against 1/16. Cold-inclusive time was 16.11 versus 105.03 s and peak MLX allocation was 1.08 versus 1.66 GiB.

*Scoring discrepancy.* The registered scorer required a lowercase prefix in the emitted listing call. The user request supplied uppercase initials and the actual callback accepts either case. The scorer gave 0/8 reference successes for each side. Replaying the saved calls under the callback’s own case normalization, still requiring the exact ordered response, correct subsequent lookup and visible version, changes this to 6/8 versus 0/8. The primary total remains 7/16 versus 0/16; the operational recount is 13/16 versus 0/16. The supplement preserves both scores per item. A separate comparison uses different tools and held-out resources, with argument equivalence specified in advance.

*Independent follow-ups on local source files.* With different tool names, argument schemas, real standard-library modules and a preregistered case-insensitive listing criterion, the model completed 12/16 dialogues against zero for Base. It solved seven of eight references to earlier observations and five of eight clarification requests. Its final answer contained the correct line count on 14/16. The two models took 13.97 and 115.77 s including their cold starts, with peaks of 1.08 and 1.66 GiB. The paired exact probability for twelve gains and zero losses was 0.000488. When asked for names starting with “A”, the module listing returned abc, aifc, antigravity, argparse and ast. Asked for the alphabetically last one's line count, the recurrent model called the tool on ast and reported its observed 1,840 lines. The sixteen dialogues use two authored interaction patterns, so the result measures transfer to new resources and tools within those patterns.

#figure(image("sql-action-audit.svg", width: 91%), caption: [First-call agreement with the publisher’s SQL operation, result column and complete filter set. The last two rows independently check the actual returned values on two table populations and the model’s completed answer. Every enrolled question is in each denominator.])

For the arithmetic and schema pointer models the first decision is a learned structured action. The runtime serializes its selected fields directly and charges that computation as first-token time. Subsequent model tokens are freely generated after executing the callback. The comparison therefore measures the entire structured system.

For the free decoder on 36 tables, 12 first calls chose the reference operation, 22 chose the reference output column and 14 reproduced the full filter set. Six matched all three. Eight produced the correct grounded answer. A different SQL expression can compute the same value on both enrolled populations, so the two execution checks and the structural audit measure different properties.

*Learned schema and literal pointers.* On 2,304 different publisher training tables, we optimized 893,166 parameters for 2,200 native updates. Preparing question and column features took 156.05 s. Optimization took 198.05 s. The first preparation attempt encountered nine training questions for which the chat template trimmed the publisher’s leading whitespace. Aligning word spans to the rendered text retained every training row and label. The final fit consumed all 2,304 examples as its sampling pool.

On 48 further human queries the model completed 14 correct grounded answers versus zero for Base (14 gains, zero losses, paired exact $p=0.000122$). It returned the correct final number on 17 versus zero. Elapsed time was 24.55 versus 261.24 s and peak MLX allocation was 1.21 versus 1.66 GiB. Its counts out of eight per operation were one lookup, two maxima, two minima, three counts, four sums and two averages. The three-per-operation requirement closed this parser. These questions differ from the free decoder's 36-table panel.

*Constraining model-written calls.* We compare two policies with the qualified typed tool weights fixed. Both generate the thinking span freely. After the closing tag, the constrained policy permits prose or a JSON call to one of the supplied tools. Once the model opens an object, llguidance 1.9.1 tracks the declarations and property order supplied for that request [17]. When the greedy token violates the grammar, MLX selects the highest-scoring admissible token from the vocabulary. On the 36 human database questions and 22 numerical tools we alternate which policy runs first and charge tokenizer conversion, grammar construction and masking to the request. The primary score still requires a correct executed result and a completed answer. Wrong table filters can satisfy the grammar.

Guidance changed 20 greedy token choices across 58 sessions. It reduced terminal invalid calls from 17 to zero and increased completed replies from 39 to 55. Grounded successes rose from 17/22 to 20/22 on public numerical functions and from 4/36 to 6/36 on the database questions. The five gains and zero losses gave exact $p=0.0625$. The gate required six net gains and $p<=0.05$. The two policies took 57.75 s guided and 47.59 s free, with 1.08 GiB peak for each. The free policy reproduced all 22 archived public trajectories. Guidance repaired a list supplied as the entire arguments object, allowing the mean function to return 87.5. On a human question about Paul Seymour's draft pick, the guided decoder supplied the round but omitted the player. It read two picks and answered with the first. The released tool model keeps its qualified free decoder.

The parser chose the publisher’s operation in 28/48 first calls, output column in 34/48 and complete filter set in 23/48. Seven matched the entire action. On the question asking how many “Years Active” listings matched “Japan 1992”, it selected the correct count and result column. It assigned “Japan 1992” to the number column and invented a second filter for “listings”. Its report of zero faithfully reflected the resulting empty selection.

*Matched conversation depth.* Both checkpoints had 802,816 trainable parameters, 53,271 supervised target tokens and 600 updates. The two-pass checkpoint reproduced the qualified system’s prompt, output-token, call and observation streams on all sixteen dialogues. Two passes completed 12/16 in 13.24 s and one pass completed 6/16 in 36.45 s after loading the weights. Peak allocations were 1,160,444,962 and 1,161,906,170 bytes. Nine gains and three losses gave paired exact $p=0.146$, missing the attribution threshold. Two passes completed seven reference dialogues against zero, while one pass solved six clarifications against five. All eight one-pass listing exchanges hit the per-turn token cap. In the ast example both models made the correct initial call and received the correct inventory. The one-pass model exhausted its reply budget after that observation. Final line-count content alone was 14/16 versus 13/16.

*Running the delivered assistant.* The four-provider offline CLI replay supplied a different real observation for each request. Across installed and isolated processes all four action, observation and output-token streams matched. Three final numbers and one version string were correct. The SQLite maximum and catalog price satisfied the single-call criterion. Before reporting the correct package version, the model requested both the dependency list and the version. For a product of 16 and 7 it submitted the invalid expression `16 * 7 = 112`, received an error and then submitted the literal `112`. The full qualification also replayed all 22 numerical sessions, answered four out of five live package questions and matched 200 shorter language streams. It retained NLL 2.982658 over 9,328 targets and answered both 131,083-token register requests in 526.79 s, with peak MLX allocation of 4.33 GiB. The supplement contains complete arguments, observations and final replies for all four providers.

== One installation on a mixed stream of user requests

We ran 50 requests in one frozen order through the qualified field release and through ordinary Base. The first request loaded the model from cold. Later requests changed between tool and text modes. The environment contained the same installed distributions and eight source files matched their enrolled hashes. Each human reading question retained all ten original documents. Both arms received the same initial prompt IDs, real callbacks, prefix-reuse policy and allowance of three turns of up to 384 tokens. Reading used one free generation. The resource timer for each request started before loading or switching models and ended after synchronization. The first-token timer included those costs.

#figure(image("mixed-deployment.svg", width:100%), caption:[One shipped system against ordinary Base on the same ordered requests. The pooled row uses the actual enrolled counts. Time and first-token time are per request. Peaks are the maximum observed MLX allocation within each family.])

The recurrent installation achieved 21/50 versus 12/50 for Base, with 12 paired gains and three losses (two-sided exact $p=0.0352$). It finished 37 versus 19 replies, used 5,362 versus 15,579 output tokens and took 83.97 versus 234.20 s. Even though whole requests finished sooner, aggregate time to the first token rose from 9.15 to 20.33 s. Peak allocation rose by 5,412,860 bytes, from 1,805,158,874 to 1,810,571,734. The first cold request took 1.41 versus 4.11 s, reaching its first token at 0.92 s in both arms.

Seven net gains came from file queries (8/8 vs 1/8) and three from SQLite (3/12 vs 0/12). Reading tied at 8/10 with identical output tokens on all ten. Arithmetic lost one net final number (2/10 vs 3/10); neither model produced a correct grounded calculator program on this panel. The candidate gave nine correct package versions versus zero, but both achieved 0/10 on the stricter one-call endpoint. In a representative case it first requested a dependency list before reading the right version. These complementary scores record both what the user learned and what the assistant did to obtain it.

Removing the file questions leaves five gains and three losses across 42 requests; removing arithmetic leaves ten gains and zero losses across 40. The supplement gives all five leave-one-family-out counts. Those calculations reuse observed timings from the original stream. This is a descriptive sensitivity analysis; the full stream remains the registered endpoint. The arithmetic loss and higher peak closed the broad promotion gate, and none of the five families improved on all four dimensions.


*Changing the requested function while holding its arguments fixed.* In each of 16 resource groups, two questions ask for different properties of the same calendar date, time zone, Unicode character or installed Python distribution. Each question in a group receives the same two real callable schemas, argument types and schema order. Eight groups use opaque identifiers. The model must read the descriptions, select the requested operation, supply the correct arguments, observe the live result and answer. Changing from an ISO weekday to a day-of-year query changes the correct operation while leaving the date unchanged. We also evaluate 24 unused ASDiv and SVAMP problems and the 22 qualified public numerical requests. Free output remains capped at 384 tokens per turn and three turns for both arms.

The candidate continues the qualified rank-32 two-pass tool adapter on 142 intent changes drawn from 284 different public demonstrations involving 40 function names. Its supervised updates also include 384 actually executed GSM8K programs, 192 real calculator error-recovery turns, 192 table-query turns and 384 older typed turns. A margin on the first divergent response token supplements likelihood. No training question uses a live evaluation resource request. We retain the complete generated program, observations, final answer and timing for each frozen session.


#figure(image("intent-performance.svg",width:96%), caption:[Required execution and final answer content for the same completed requests. The two scores coincide for some sources. Base correctly solved several word problems without performing a valid tool call.])

The 802,816 trainable parameters received 1,200 updates and 229,609 supervised positions. Preparing frozen inputs took 203.92 s and optimization took 377.17 s. The trained model achieved 45/78 grounded successes versus 0/78 for untouched Base (45 gains, zero losses, paired exact $p=5.68 times 10^(-14)$). It gave 47 correct final answers versus 22 and completed 68 replies versus 32. It used 3,865 versus 22,640 output tokens, took 54.56 versus 333.15 s and peaked at 1.08 versus 1.66 GiB. Summed request first-token time was 7.25 versus 6.83 s. The pooled item probability is conditional on this benchmark composition and repeated use of the public numerical panel.

On 32 real resource requests the model executed and answered 20 correctly versus zero, including both requested properties on six of 16 shared resources versus none (paired $p=0.03125$). Five of those six pairs had opaque function names. Each naming condition had only eight resources and used different resources. The other successes comprised 19/22 public numerical calls and six of 24 grounded word problems. The adapter retained every one of the 17 previously correct public calls. It repaired two formerly invalid calls, including a sports average of 87.5. On the ordinal date and weekday of 26 November 2026, the installed command executed the two requested operations on the same ISO date and returned 330 and 4. Asked for the daylight-saving contribution in Paris, it called the total-offset operation and gave 120 minutes instead of 60. All three installed action, observation and token streams reproduced the model-study archive.

The ordinary model gave eight correct final ASDiv numbers and six correct SVAMP numbers, all without a successful grounded trajectory. The recurrent calculator answered five and one correctly on those sources. This missed the per-source arithmetic requirement and closed default promotion and the matched-depth successor. The tool weights and the actual read-only providers remain available through the installed command for explicit use.


In the 24 human arithmetic requests the two-pass model obtained a numerical callback on 20. It copied the final returned number exactly on all 20, including 14 incorrect programs. Three further callbacks raised errors and one request had no callback. On the meatball subtraction problem the correct answer, 14, appeared as the first intermediate result. The model added an unnecessary operation and reported the final value, −11. Base made no successful callback and independently supplied 14 correct final quantities. These records locate the larger loss in selecting and stopping the calculation. The supplementary CSV includes every completed call and return.

*Learning a tool call from the model's own reasoning.* We froze a distinct primary comparison on 24 ASDiv and 24 SVAMP questions. Each fit uses 256 GSM8K and 256 MAWPS programs whose actual calculator result equals the human target. Training and evaluation use the same deployed calculator schema. For the first turn the ordinary 8-bit-prefix decoder emits up to 96 of its own greedy thinking tokens. A shared BF16 final attention layer with rank-48 adapters then processes the entire causal prefix twice at that layer and generates the action. Every draft token counts toward the same 384-token allowance given to untouched Base. At training time the two-pass finisher learns from the actual ordinary prefix followed by a worked operation sequence and call, and learns the final answer after the live callback. At test time it receives the question, generated history and tool return. We record the final quantity, completed grounding, strict numeric text, elapsed time, first-token latency and memory for every item. The experimental treatment differs in both training and inference schedule. Yao et al. [34] previously studied interleaved reasoning, actions and observations.

#figure(image("draft-program.svg",width:92%),caption:[Free final numerical answers and completed calculator-grounded answers on new ASDiv and SVAMP questions. The ordinary-prefix and action model share backbone weights.])

The ordinary model generated 48,726 training draft tokens. Of 512 drafts, 490 reached the 96-token boundary. Preparation took 602.25 s and the 960 optimizer updates took 325.79 s for 1,204,224 parameters. Across the 48 test questions, the candidate completed 48 answers and Base completed 31. All 28 correct candidate answers included an actual arithmetic callback returning the reference quantity. Base completed no such calls yet produced 29 correct final numbers, including 25 strict numerical replies. Paired final-answer gains and losses were 10 and 11 (exact $p=1$). ASDiv tied 17/24 and SVAMP favored Base 12/24 versus 11/24. The candidate took 77.13 versus 166.59 s and used 1.08 versus 1.66 GiB. Summed latency to the first generated token of each user request was 4.177 versus 4.160 s.

Asked about packaging 50 cupcakes after five were eaten, the ordinary draft explicitly concluded nine. The emitted program computed only 50 minus five, and the final answer faithfully repeated the resulting 45. On a comparison between departed and remaining restaurant customers, the decoder likewise stopped after computing the number who left. A numeric callback certifies the value of the emitted expression. Its agreement with a human reference also cannot prove that every intermediate operation expresses the intended reasoning. The final-number gate closed both deployment and matched training for this fit.

*Learning when to ask for a missing argument.* When2Call provides externally generated preferred and rejected responses to tool requests [39]. We start from the qualified two-pass numerical tool model. Direct preference optimization adjusts a model’s relative likelihood of both responses against the frozen starting model [40]. We train on 384 external questions and reserve 48 distinct questions from the same source. The two source splits can share schemas. Each minibatch includes two response pairs and one original tool-training response. We sum full response log probabilities in the preference loss and normalize the auxiliary chosen-response and replay losses by their token counts. Inference uses ordinary greedy generation with no imposed action grammar.

For each of 16 queries over four unmodified CSV files, we enroll both a complete user request and a conversation that withholds the file, data row or column. The follow-up supplies the omitted value, even if a model guessed. A whole-pair success requires asking for the missing input, making the single correct call in each applicable exchange, and returning the exact scalar in both conversations. The questions are project-authored. Eight anchors query published Antarctic penguin observations [41], and eight query this project’s recorded measurements. The code reads each quoted UTF-8 CSV from disk. We also rerun all 22 older public numerical requests, keeping their original scorer and callback implementations.

Across the fitting preferences and original replay responses, the audit found 552 distinct normalized user questions. No initial file request or follow-up shares a sequence of five words with these questions, and neither CSV operation name occurs in their tool schemas. Removing numerical distinctions reduces the 32 initial file requests to 30 distinct forms and the 16 follow-ups to nine. The artificial variation is concentrated in four files. The 48 held-out source preferences have no exact question matches, but their maximum five-word Jaccard similarity to fitting questions is 0.429. These comparisons quantify overlap with additional training. The original checkpoint’s pretraining remains unobservable.

*Interleaving calculation and evidence from complete reports.* FinQA contains expert-annotated numerical programs grounded in financial reports [42]. TabMWP contains school mathematics questions over tables [43]. Toolformer studied models learning when to invoke external operations and condition subsequent text on their returns [44]. Here we supervise the next arithmetic action using a separate live calculator response after each operation. Each training dialogue ends with the human numerical answer. The first financial-report turn can use up to 48 tokens from an annotated supporting line. The GSM8K dialogues use the source rationale leading into each operation. The 256 complete fitting report excerpts, 384 human arithmetic solutions and 192 typed-tool replay replies yield 2,493 assistant targets. Fitting uses the qualified two-pass tool weights, rank 32, 1,200 AdamW updates and four targets per update.

We withhold 24 corporate issuers entirely from the local fit and evaluate one full report excerpt per issuer. Both models also receive 24 distinct free-response tables from TabMWP, a source absent from the local fit. Each enrolled financial program executes to the published answer within its recorded precision. The reports, tables and questions go into the prompt. Expert programs, answers and supporting-line labels are reserved for scoring. Both arms receive identical initial IDs, up to five assistant turns of 384 tokens each, and access to the same live calculator. The primary score credits a correct final number, including a number in prose; separate columns record strict numerical text, completion, correct answers backed by a substantive call, and a subsequent operation using an earlier return. Operand equality is observational evidence of reuse. For rounded financial answers we respect half of the final printed unit. The frozen decision requires at least six net correct answers, an exact paired probability at most 0.05, gains in both sources and retention of strict answers, completion, grounding and resource limits.

The fitting arithmetic questions and original tool replay contain 808 distinct normalized user texts. Exact matches with both evaluation sources were zero. After masking numerical distinctions, maximum five-word Jaccard similarity was 0.192 for financial questions and 0.053 for school tables. The actual tokenizer produced 722–2,335 initial tokens per financial excerpt and 276–326 per school-table request.
